and I struggle to consume,

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:26.328
and there would be a lot of efficiencies

00:03:26.328 --> 00:03:29.508
if I was better at consuming,
so kind of that ability

00:03:29.508 --> 00:03:33.372
to concentrate on things
that are not that important.

00:03:33.372 --> 00:03:34.989
Take that the way it was meant, guys,

00:03:34.989 --> 00:03:36.567
which is, ya know, I can concentrate

00:03:36.567 --> 00:03:38.704
on a one, two percent kind of variable,

00:03:38.704 --> 00:03:41.560
but I struggle when I
think things are important,

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:44.231
but not that important.

00:03:44.231 --> 00:03:46.713
- [Voiceover] Amanda asks,
"What ways can agencies

00:03:46.713 --> 00:03:50.220
"make staff meetings more productive?"

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:53.051
- Amanda, I love this question.

00:03:53.051 --> 00:03:54.702
By the way, I just took
my wedding ring off.

00:03:54.702 --> 00:03:55.513
Big week for me.

00:03:55.513 --> 00:03:59.484
Turned 39 and had my 10th
wedding anniversary this week.

00:03:59.484 --> 00:04:00.714
Thursday is the wedding anniversary,

00:04:00.714 --> 00:04:02.102
Friday is my birthday.

00:04:02.102 --> 00:04:04.477
That's right, at midnight at my wedding,

00:04:04.477 --> 00:04:07.147
the whole wedding party
sang me Happy Birthday.

00:04:07.147 --> 00:04:08.238
That's how I roll.

00:04:08.238 --> 00:04:10.270
Amanda, Amanda right?

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:12.905
Amanda, great question.

00:04:12.905 --> 00:04:15.365
The best way to make
meetings more efficient

00:04:15.365 --> 00:04:16.969
is to cut them in half.

00:04:16.969 --> 00:04:18.570
To cut them in,

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:20.519
to cut seven eighths out of them.

00:04:20.519 --> 00:04:22.611
The amount of bullcrap
that goes on in a meeting,

00:04:22.611 --> 00:04:24.221
the set-up, this and that,

00:04:24.221 --> 00:04:25.367
one thing I've just noticed,

00:04:25.367 --> 00:04:28.565
I hired a couple of very
senior executives recently,

00:04:28.565 --> 00:04:31.730
three or four SVPs, very
senior people here at Vayner,

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:35.027
and to a tee, each of
them have come up to me

00:04:35.027 --> 00:04:38.255
and said, "Wow, your
meetings are so weird.

00:04:38.255 --> 00:04:39.764
"They're five minutes,
they're ten minutes,

00:04:39.764 --> 00:04:41.319
"they're 15 minutes."

00:04:41.319 --> 00:04:43.478
Because most of it doesn't matter.

00:04:43.478 --> 00:04:44.686
This is becoming the theme of this show.

00:04:44.686 --> 00:04:46.417
It just doesn't matter.

00:04:46.417 --> 00:04:48.540
So I think one of the best
ways to make it efficient

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:50.537
is to cut them in time.

00:04:50.537 --> 00:04:51.674
One of the things that I realized

00:04:51.674 --> 00:04:54.879
is that people will fit 10 pounds of crap

00:04:54.879 --> 00:04:56.480
into a 10 pound bag.

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:59.519
You give them a 15 pound bag,
that's what they fit it into.

00:04:59.519 --> 00:05:00.683
They don't overfill.

00:05:00.683 --> 00:05:03.076
What I mean by that is, if
you have a one hour meeting,

00:05:03.076 --> 00:05:04.306
if we have a one hour meeting,

00:05:04.306 --> 00:05:07.510
we'll fit what we need to
get into that into that hour.

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:09.994
We'll banter a little bit,
we'll go a little bit deeper,

00:05:09.994 --> 00:05:12.224
but if we cut that same
meeting to 15 minutes,

00:05:12.224 --> 00:05:15.382
we'll still accomplish that,
and we've saved the 45.

00:05:15.382 --> 00:05:18.245
So the answer to your
question is restrictions.

00:05:18.245 --> 00:05:21.929
Create a mandate for the
length of the meeting.

00:05:21.929 --> 00:05:23.833
- Hi Gary, my name's Brent Wampler,

00:05:23.833 --> 00:05:26.507
and I was actually born in
Russia like you were as a baby.

00:05:26.507 --> 00:05:29.123
I'm 14 years old, and here's my question.

00:05:29.123 --> 00:05:30.938
I wanna be an entrepreneur
when I get older,

00:05:30.938 --> 00:05:32.145
but I don't know where to start.

00:05:32.145 --> 00:05:35.123
Like, what actions should I
be taking right now as a kid?

00:05:35.123 --> 00:05:35.930
Thanks.

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:37.161
- B, listen to me.

00:05:37.161 --> 00:05:39.505
First and foremost, by
asking this question

00:05:39.505 --> 00:05:41.781
and knowing what The #AskGaryVee Show is,

00:05:41.781 --> 00:05:44.243
you're putting yourself in a
position to be an entrepreneur.

00:05:44.243 --> 00:05:45.612
I like that.

00:05:45.612 --> 00:05:46.913
What I don't like is the question

00:05:46.913 --> 00:05:48.097
because what you should know

00:05:48.097 --> 00:05:49.699
if you're a purebred entrepreneur,

00:05:49.699 --> 00:05:51.603
so wanting to be an entrepreneur versus

00:05:51.603 --> 00:05:53.461
being an entrepreneur are
two very different things,

00:05:53.461 --> 00:05:55.365
and I have no interest
in giving the medicine

00:05:55.365 --> 00:05:57.524
to a 14-year-old, especially
'cause I gave the medicine

00:05:57.524 --> 00:05:59.747
to a 14-year-old Steeler's fan yesterday,

00:05:59.747 --> 00:06:01.796
and it wasn't pretty
and I'm not proud of it.

00:06:01.796 --> 00:06:04.397
On this show is probably the
second most competitive place

00:06:04.397 --> 00:06:06.510
I live in, and so what I
want to tell you is this.

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:08.437
Look, if I were you, I would sell

00:06:08.437 --> 00:06:10.898
that Under Armour sweatshirt
that you're wearing

00:06:10.898 --> 00:06:13.341
in the video to some other
kid in the neighborhood.

00:06:13.341 --> 00:06:15.821
I would go back in the woods
in the video that you just had

00:06:15.821 --> 00:06:19.999
and find some rocks and sell
'em to some nine-year-old girl.

00:06:19.999 --> 00:06:20.858
That's what I did.

00:06:20.858 --> 00:06:21.811
I was that raw.

00:06:21.811 --> 00:06:23.343
Now, we're not all the same.

00:06:23.343 --> 00:06:25.410
What I'm trying to tell you is

00:06:25.410 --> 00:06:28.324
the best way to become something
is to act like something.

00:06:28.324 --> 00:06:30.002
So, you wanna be an entrepreneur?

00:06:30.002 --> 00:06:31.187
Start acting like one,

00:06:31.187 --> 00:06:33.926
meaning start a business,
start selling things.

00:06:33.926 --> 00:06:36.899
Both will work, or find a mentor.

00:06:36.899 --> 00:06:41.078
Find the 18, 19, 20,
21-year-old kind of entrepreneur

00:06:41.078 --> 00:06:44.445
in your neighborhood and
start helping her or him out

00:06:44.445 --> 00:06:47.648
for free just to learn the
hustle, to taste the game.

00:06:47.648 --> 00:06:49.669
You've gotta put yourself in the position.

00:06:49.669 --> 00:06:51.983
There's no reading about entrepreneurship.

00:06:51.983 --> 00:06:54.406
There was a question today that
came through for #AskGaryVee

00:06:54.406 --> 00:06:56.295
that said, "Gary, name the first,

00:06:56.295 --> 00:06:58.965
"name the four best business
books you've read this year."

00:06:58.965 --> 00:07:00.629
And I laughed my ass off because

00:07:00.629 --> 00:07:02.207
I don't think I've read
four books in my life,

00:07:02.207 --> 00:07:04.181
and definitely not four business books,

00:07:04.181 --> 00:07:06.084
and so there's no reading, my man.

00:07:06.084 --> 00:07:10.760
There's doing, and so sell
the shirt off your back.

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:13.422
- [Voiceover] TJ asks, "As
a fellow son of immigrants,

00:07:13.422 --> 00:07:15.767
"when you were 25, how
did you overcome fear

00:07:15.767 --> 00:07:19.739
"of not doing as much or
not having it as hard?"

00:07:19.739 --> 00:07:21.490
- TJ, this is a great question.

00:07:21.490 --> 00:07:23.917
In life, there's certain
things that you can control

00:07:23.917 --> 00:07:25.868
and other things that you can't.

00:07:25.868 --> 00:07:29.304
One of the things that
really stand out to me

00:07:29.304 --> 00:07:30.575
is how much I,

00:07:30.575 --> 00:07:32.136
this is something I've rarely shared.

00:07:32.136 --> 00:07:33.250
I think I've shared it once or twice,

00:07:33.250 --> 00:07:34.337
Stunwin, keep me honest here.

00:07:34.337 --> 00:07:36.345
I'd be curious if you know.

00:07:36.345 --> 00:07:37.890
I think I've talked
about it once or twice.

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:40.954
I often, when I was 18 years old,

00:07:40.954 --> 00:07:43.022
would call my mom while I was in college

00:07:43.022 --> 00:07:46.597
and cry, literally cry, like a baby,

00:07:46.597 --> 00:07:49.583
get that emotional about the fear that,

00:07:49.583 --> 00:07:50.638
at that point, I thought I was going

00:07:50.638 --> 00:07:52.030
into the family business, right?

00:07:52.030 --> 00:07:53.493
I was in a crap college.

00:07:53.493 --> 00:07:54.237
I was all in.

00:07:54.237 --> 00:07:56.472
I was going home every weekend working,

00:07:56.472 --> 00:07:59.507
and I had a huge fear
that I was never gonna get

00:07:59.507 --> 00:08:01.642
the credit I deserved as being,

00:08:01.642 --> 00:08:03.662
this is, ya know, the confidence inside,

00:08:03.662 --> 00:08:05.591
by going into my dad's business

00:08:05.591 --> 00:08:08.150
because everybody would always
say that Sasha gave it to me,

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:10.862
that my par--
look there's even in Crush It!,

00:08:10.862 --> 00:08:13.645
there's even negative
reviews on Amazon of like,

00:08:13.645 --> 00:08:15.319
"Oh yeah, but his dad gave him

00:08:15.319 --> 00:08:16.782
"a 3 million dollar liquor store."

00:08:16.782 --> 00:08:18.796
Now, you know, as,

00:08:18.796 --> 00:08:20.009
I underestimated people's knowledge

00:08:20.009 --> 00:08:21.774
that a three million
dollar revenue liquor store

00:08:21.774 --> 00:08:24.223
is not a three million dollar grant,

00:08:24.223 --> 00:08:25.372
and things of that nature,

00:08:25.372 --> 00:08:27.254
and then obviously my skills.

00:08:27.254 --> 00:08:28.252
I said recently in an episode,

00:08:28.252 --> 00:08:29.784
a lot of you caught it and tweeted it,

00:08:29.784 --> 00:08:31.201
"The truth is undefeated."

00:08:31.201 --> 00:08:33.163
If I was gonna be a great businessman,

00:08:33.163 --> 00:08:35.542
I would get the credit that
I desperately, clearly wanted

00:08:35.542 --> 00:08:37.121
at that age and still at this point do

00:08:37.121 --> 00:08:39.582
'cause the narrative does matter to me,

00:08:39.582 --> 00:08:43.240
not just the dollar results.

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:45.527
And so, it's interesting,

00:08:45.527 --> 00:08:47.245
this question really struck a chord

00:08:47.245 --> 00:08:49.451
because I remember not
only having that feeling,

00:08:49.451 --> 00:08:50.705
but I had a separate feeling

00:08:50.705 --> 00:08:52.377
which is the answer to your question.

00:08:52.377 --> 00:08:54.374
The answer to your
question is very simple.

00:08:54.374 --> 00:08:55.901
One, I knew that I would never have it

00:08:55.901 --> 00:08:58.646
as tough as my parents, so what I did was

00:08:58.646 --> 00:09:02.222
I had to execute against that privilege.

00:09:02.222 --> 00:09:03.824
Executing against your privilege

00:09:03.824 --> 00:09:05.866
is the only thing that you can do.

00:09:05.866 --> 00:09:08.886
I remember two things
that stand out for me.

00:09:08.886 --> 00:09:11.243
One, growing that family business.

00:09:11.243 --> 00:09:14.389
I wanted to pay back my
parents as quickly as possible.

00:09:14.389 --> 00:09:17.732
I, the greatest two
feelings I have in my body

00:09:17.732 --> 00:09:19.473
as an entrepreneur, answer this question.

00:09:19.473 --> 00:09:22.212
One, the fact that me and
my parents walk around

00:09:22.212 --> 00:09:24.698
and all feel like we've
given to each other.

00:09:24.698 --> 00:09:25.998
I paid back, right?

00:09:25.998 --> 00:09:27.715
I built such a big business
that it helped them

00:09:27.715 --> 00:09:29.922
as much as it helped
me getting that at bat.

00:09:29.922 --> 00:09:33.196
And number two, the day my
dad a couple of years ago

00:09:33.196 --> 00:09:35.007
maybe 18 months ago, said to me

00:09:35.007 --> 00:09:36.516
that I work harder than him,

00:09:36.516 --> 00:09:37.654
and that was a good feeling.

00:09:37.654 --> 00:09:39.697
That was something I wanted

00:09:39.697 --> 00:09:42.757
because that was another way
to pay back my privilege.

00:09:42.757 --> 00:09:46.965
The fact that my dad sacrificed
his entire life working

00:09:46.965 --> 00:09:49.240
to set up the foundation
where our family is going

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:51.352
is something that I wanted to
pay back by doing the same.

00:09:51.352 --> 00:09:53.604
Now look, to pay that
back, I also want to have

00:09:53.604 --> 00:09:55.742
a better work life balance, to
spend more time with my kids,

00:09:55.742 --> 00:09:57.251
something he wasn't able to do.

00:09:57.251 --> 00:09:59.803
So there's other things
besides just working hard,

00:09:59.803 --> 00:10:02.707
but the fact of the matter
is the only thing you can do

00:10:02.707 --> 00:10:06.144
is to execute against your privilege.

00:10:06.144 --> 00:10:08.744
- [Voiceover] Joy asks,
"What social media techniques

00:10:08.744 --> 00:10:12.489
"do you think work best
for promoting a book?"

00:10:14.409 --> 00:10:15.826
- Joy, I was excited about
answering this question

